{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And Abram said: Adonai Elohim, what will You give me</b> — behold, You have saved me from the kings, but You have not assured me regarding [having issue from] my wife, only You said that You would greatly multiply my reward, but what will my reward be without children? Now, it did not occur to his mind that this great reward might be the World to Come, for that requires no assurance, since every servant of Elohim will find life before Him. But in this world there are righteous men upon whom the deeds of the wicked befall, therefore they require assurance. Moreover, 'exceedingly' (הרבה מאד) — meaning that he will merit two tables [of reward] in all the good fitting for the perfect righteous, without any punishment whatsoever. Moreover, the assurance was concerning what he was afraid of, and He returned and explained to him His assurance so that he would not fear even this, for He would make his seed as the stars of heaven for multitude. Now you must ask: for it was already said to Abraham (Genesis 13:15), 'for all the land that you see, to you I will give it and to your seed forever, and I will make your seed as the dust of the earth,' so how can he now say, 'but I go childless' and 'behold, the son of my house inherits me,' and why did he not believe in the first prophecy as he will believe in this one? And the answer is that the righteous do not rely on themselves lest they sin inadvertently, as it is written (Jeremiah 18:9), 'At the moment I speak concerning a nation and concerning a kingdom, to build and to plant, and that nation turns back and does evil before Me, then I relent concerning the good.' Now, he saw himself growing old and his prophecy not being fulfilled, and he thought that his sins had withheld the good. And perhaps he now feared lest he be punished on account of the lives he had slain, according to the words of our Rabbis (Genesis Rabbah 44:5). And in this same manner they said in Bereshit Rabbah (76:2) concerning 'and Jacob was greatly afraid and was distressed' (Genesis 32:8): from this we learn that there is no assurance to the righteous in this world, and so forth." "<b>What will You give me, and I go childless</b> — Ibn Ezra explained it as: I am dying without children, in the sense of 'for man goes to his eternal home' (Ecclesiastes 12:5). But the correct interpretation in my eyes is that at first he complains: what will my reward be, given that I have no children, and I go wandering and roaming in a foreign land, alone like a juniper in the desert — none going out and none coming into my house besides Eliezer, a foreign man whom I took for myself from Damascus, not from my father's house and not from my land. And afterward he said, 'Behold, to me You have not given seed,' as You promised me, 'and behold, the son of my house mentioned above inherits me, for I have grown old and my time has come without seed, and behold, I am stricken and my reward in which You promised me at first is lost.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "Resolved 'הכרת' as 'my wife' (i.e., Sarah), following the context of the midrashic reading Ramban adopts; the Hebrew is elliptical, literally 'from the cutting [off],' but here clearly referring to issue from his wife.", 1 "In the final clause, read 'ענוש' as 'stricken' (from ענש, punished/afflicted); the Hebrew is terse and I have rendered it hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:21:17.082508939Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}